{"h": {"measure": "R", "k": 100}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 103, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 0->3 already absent, removing 2->3 leaves only edge 4 to reach the output, so the cell can become disconnected or very weak (see experiments 3 and 95)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing a parameter-free pool on the final edge with a learnable conv adds capacity to the output path, which gives large gains (see experiments 33 and 83)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "If edge 2->3 is missing, adding 1->3 can reconnect the computation from node 1 to the output (see experiment 65)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When 2->3 is absent, a conv on the direct input->output edge may be the only effective learnable path (see experiment 96)."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Without the direct 0->3 edge, adding a conv path through node 2 strongly improves connectivity and capacity (see experiment 76)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 0->2 absent, removing 0->1 cuts off both intermediate nodes and leaves only edge 3 (see experiments 51 and 5)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Removing the direct edge when 2->3 is also absent leaves the output fed only by edge 4, which risks disconnection."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Swapping the strongest conv on the 0->2 edge for pooling removes key learnable capacity (see experiments 18 and 79)."}, {